<DocsLayout title="Art Bible">
  <h1>Art Bible</h1>
  <p>The full Art Bible — visual reference for illustrators, UI designers, and anyone building imagery for the setting — lives in <code>fates-edge-docs/ART_BIBLE.md</code>. It's long (500+ lines, region-by-region), so it stays in the docs repo as the source of truth rather than being duplicated here. This page is the orientation.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Read it here:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/Chronophage-net/fates-edge-docs/blob/main/ART_BIBLE.md">github.com/Chronophage-net/fates-edge-docs/ART_BIBLE.md</a>
  </div>

  <h2>What's in it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Core visual thesis</strong> — genre touchstones, the "one unifying image" (a mosaic of splintered nations in a dead empire's shell), and camera language.</li>
    <li><strong>Region-by-region visual identity</strong> — touchstone, palette, architecture, dress/status markers, and a taboo/secret visual hook for every culture in the gazetteer.</li>
    <li><strong>Three rules that apply everywhere:</strong> nothing is new (draw wear and repair, not pristine objects), power always costs something and should show, and no chosen ones or perfect kingdoms.</li>
    <li>A setting-wide motif: almost every culture counts to eight and dreads a ninth — use it as a background visual habit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>For UI and web-client work</h2>
  <p>The Art Bible governs illustration and world imagery. For interface colors, spacing, and components (this portal, the blog, the web client), see <a href="/design-tokens">Design Tokens</a> instead — the two are kept in agreement (gold as a "something has a price attached" accent shows up in both) but serve different jobs.</p>
</DocsLayout>
